issue_repro_game.probe has two false-green channels: a build that never ran reads identically to a clean one, and a location-less engine error is invisible to every error bucket


## Found by lane-resolver during B096 claim 2, 2026-08-31

**Every probe lane inherits these.** They turn "the build never ran" into ACCEPT
on questions whose entire subject is acceptance. That is the worst possible
polarity: the failure mode is indistinguishable from the answer.

Caught only because a fixture with a deliberately WRONG dependency id scored
ACCEPT. Nothing else in the run looked wrong.

## Channel 1 — `fleet.read_log_window` drops every `[ TRAC]` line

`Loaded plugin '<id>'` is a TRAC line. With TRAC filtered out, `compiled_clean`
degenerates to "no ERR attributed to me", and **a build that never ran reads
identically to a clean one.**

## Channel 2 — `fleet.parse_game_diagnostics` only matches located diagnostics

The regex expects `path (line, col) : SEV : msg`. An engine error with no source
location matches nothing, so it is invisible to `game_errors`, to
`foreign_errors`, AND to `unattributed_errors` — which means `contended` stays
False and no guard fires anywhere.

The concrete miss: `Couldn't find required plugin dependency "..."` followed by
`Loaded plugin` scored a clean ACCEPT.

## Channel 3 — `Loaded plugin` is logged even after `Script compilation failed!`

So the presence of `Loaded plugin` is **necessary but not sufficient**, and any
guard that treats it as sufficient inherits the same false green. Do not fix
channel 1 by simply un-filtering TRAC and testing for `Loaded plugin`.

## The fix lane-resolver validated locally, and deliberately did not commit

It modified NO shared harness file, so no other lane in flight was disturbed —
correct call, and the reason this is a ticket rather than a merge:

1. Unfiltered log window.
2. **Build-bracketed** `raw_build_errors` — slice the window by the build's own
   start/end markers rather than by severity.
3. `BUILD-ERR` kept DISTINCT from `REJECT`. "The engine refused this construct"
   and "this build did not happen" are different answers and collapsing them is
   what produced the original defect.

## Test axes

The bug is a MISSING signal, so a red run cannot validate the test by itself —
see the hazards catalogue on absences. Build positive controls that assert each
channel can distinguish its pair:

- A build that never ran vs. a build that ran clean. Must differ.
- A located engine error vs. a location-less one. Both must reach a bucket.
- A compile failure followed by `Loaded plugin` vs. a real clean load. Must
  differ.
- A foreign plugin's error in the same window vs. our own. Attribution must
  hold, and this is the one that already works — keep it as the control that
  proves the harness is wired at all.

Each pair must be measured, not asserted. A guard that fires on both halves of a
pair is as broken as one that fires on neither.